                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "59a1312e-1a68-48ce-9343-95f33f253b12",
                        "source_entry_id": "114e3ad3-22ff-37f3-bbd7-bd8fc93a767c",
                        "source_sense_id": "ac637480-9125-38fa-baef-a10b2ce6a550",
                        "target_lang_word": "epistle",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "epistle",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "22ab0d43-34b5-3262-a5b2-f1a9a6827db5",
                                        "entry_id": "2f1c175f-b608-30f7-9dea-53da9177f416",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A literary composition in the form of a letter or series of letters, especially one in verse."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "17710d89-c6a4-48b5-8999-84e45051ab55",
                                                "sense_id": "22ab0d43-34b5-3262-a5b2-f1a9a6827db5",
                                                "text": "[Y]our Grace ſhal herein perceiue a nevv faſhion of diſcourſe, by Epistles; nevv to our language, uſual to others: and (as Noueltie is neuer vvithout ſome plea of vſe) more free, more familiar."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "52f1ada8-9e0d-43e1-8fe0-b3b827964414",
                                                "sense_id": "22ab0d43-34b5-3262-a5b2-f1a9a6827db5",
                                                "text": "For in this Addreſs to your Lordſhip, I deſign not a Treatiſe of Heroick Poetry, but vvrite in a looſe Epiſtolary vvay, ſomevvhat tending to that Subject, after the Example of Horace, in his Firſt Epiſtle of the Second Book to Auguſtus Cæſar, and of that to the Piſo’s, vvhich vve call his Art of Poetry."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "88db5584-e3b4-4686-862f-d114152d55cc",
                                                "sense_id": "22ab0d43-34b5-3262-a5b2-f1a9a6827db5",
                                                "text": "But let our poet, while he writes epistles, though never so familiar, still remember that he writes in verse, and must for that reason have a more than ordinary care not to fall into prose and a vulgar diction, excepting where the nature and humour of the thing does necessarily require it."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "421a9e43-9030-4642-a752-cc13f6cd244e",
                                                "sense_id": "22ab0d43-34b5-3262-a5b2-f1a9a6827db5",
                                                "text": "In all Compoſitions of Genius, therefore, 'tis requiſite that the VVriter have ſome Plan or Object; and tho' he may be hurry'd from this Plan by the Vehemence of Thought, as in an Ode, or drop it careleſly, as in an Epiſtle or Eſſay, there muſt appear ſome Aim or Intention, in his firſt ſetting out, if not in the Compoſition of the vvhole VVork."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "6c9d4c79-b1c6-3966-83a2-ff9c410fc26a",
                                        "entry_id": "2f1c175f-b608-30f7-9dea-53da9177f416",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A letter, especially one which is formal or issued publicly."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "humorous",
                                            "literary"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "5fbecdb1-98b3-45b0-9f7e-fc4e88eae17a",
                                                "sense_id": "6c9d4c79-b1c6-3966-83a2-ff9c410fc26a",
                                                "text": "After that John Frauncis [i.e., Giovanni Francesco Pico della Mirandola], the neuiew of Picꝰ [Picus], had (as it appeareth in the firſt epiſtle of Picus to him) begon a chaunge in his liuyng: […]"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "43ecc71b-825d-4119-946a-bfd1ccc94518",
                                                "sense_id": "6c9d4c79-b1c6-3966-83a2-ff9c410fc26a",
                                                "text": "Plato alſo, that diuine Philoſopher, hath many Godly medicines agaynſt the poyſon of vayne pleaſure, in many places, but ſpecially in his Epistles to Dioniſius the tyrant of Sicilie: […]"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "ae58d723-3d7b-49d6-a460-48d422735290",
                                                "sense_id": "6c9d4c79-b1c6-3966-83a2-ff9c410fc26a",
                                                "text": "I vvill drop in his vvay ſome obſcure Epiſtles of loue, vvherein by the colour of his beard, the ſhape of his legge, the manner of his gate, the expreſſure of his eye, forehead, and complection, he ſhall finde himſelfe moſt feelingly perſonated."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "7c9978a3-8576-46d2-91e1-57df46cbc99d",
                                                "sense_id": "6c9d4c79-b1c6-3966-83a2-ff9c410fc26a",
                                                "text": "[I]n publiſhing theſe Epiſtles at this Time, you pleaſe to ſay, That I have done like Hezekiah, vvhen he ſhevved his Treaſures to the Babylonians, that I have diſcovered my Riches to Thieves, vvho vvill bind me faſt and ſhare my Goods. To this I anſvver, that if thoſe innocent Letters (for I knovv none of them but is ſuch) fall among ſuch Thieves, they vvill have no great prize to carry avvay, it vvill be but petty larceny: […]"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "cddd9d8a-3f77-48fe-aa44-acf734775a32",
                                                "sense_id": "6c9d4c79-b1c6-3966-83a2-ff9c410fc26a",
                                                "text": "The moſt authentic account of the education and adventures of Julian, is contained in the epiſtle or manifeſto vvhich he himſelf addreſſed to the ſenate and people of Athens."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "53095aa3-65c9-4fe5-b9f1-f5b9775b0323",
                                                "sense_id": "6c9d4c79-b1c6-3966-83a2-ff9c410fc26a",
                                                "text": "At this moment his page entered with a packet. \"Lights!\" said Evelyn, carelessly—for, as our readers will have already divined, he was the melancholy soliloquist—\"I may as well read the Cardinal's epistle at once;—but I am no tool for his purpose."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "3ea434b8-8948-4627-b398-c003d0e32750",
                                                "sense_id": "6c9d4c79-b1c6-3966-83a2-ff9c410fc26a",
                                                "text": "Wide flew the doors, and lo, / Messire de Beringhen, and this epistle!"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "29fb1c19-be08-444e-91f9-d5ee6c215954",
                                                "sense_id": "6c9d4c79-b1c6-3966-83a2-ff9c410fc26a",
                                                "text": "Then one morning there came an enigmatic note slipped into our letterbox. \"Dear me, Mr. Holmes. Dear me!\" said this singular epistle. There was neither superscription nor signature. I laughed at the quaint message; but Holmes showed unwonted seriousness."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "3f715cbe-47d5-391a-b349-c6d90528f262",
                                        "entry_id": "2f1c175f-b608-30f7-9dea-53da9177f416",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A letter, especially one which is formal or issued publicly.",
                                            "Chiefly with a qualifying word, as in epistle dedicatory: a letter of dedication addressed to a patron or reader published as a preface to a literary work."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "historical",
                                            "humorous",
                                            "literary",
                                            "specifically"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "120f183e-5954-4043-a2c2-5700f23648d9",
                                                "sense_id": "3f715cbe-47d5-391a-b349-c6d90528f262",
                                                "text": "[I]n the Epiſtle Dedicatorie thereof to a great man of this land, whom he calls his verie right honourable good Lord, he recounteth his large bounties beſtowed upon him, and talkes of the ſecret favours which hee did him in his ſtudie or cloſet at court."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "4f26893f-81b6-4066-abc1-14421ac5d1f0",
                                                "sense_id": "3f715cbe-47d5-391a-b349-c6d90528f262",
                                                "text": "[L]eſt a longer Epiſtle may diminiſh your pleaſure, I ſhal not adventure to make this Epiſtle longer then to add this follovving truth, That I am really, Sir, Your moſt affectionate Friend, and moſt humble Servant, Iz[aak] Wa[lton]."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "35a49cc1-ad22-30cd-b694-e62194c607eb",
                                        "entry_id": "2f1c175f-b608-30f7-9dea-53da9177f416",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "One of the books of the New Testament which was originally a letter issued by an apostle to an individual or a community."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "a37b6985-da24-4126-ac5d-93acbd98bbfc",
                                                "sense_id": "35a49cc1-ad22-30cd-b694-e62194c607eb",
                                                "text": "The Evangiles and Acts, teach us vvhat to beleeve, but the Epiſtles of the Apoſtles vvhat to do."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "01a1c2df-dc00-4b61-be90-e2124b9754f7",
                                                "sense_id": "35a49cc1-ad22-30cd-b694-e62194c607eb",
                                                "text": "[T]he Doctrine of Faith, and Myſtery of Salvation, vvas more fully explained, in the Epiſtles vvrit by the Apoſtles."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "3883a56c-1d1c-4b6d-a9e7-e44ae56dbf5f",
                                                "sense_id": "35a49cc1-ad22-30cd-b694-e62194c607eb",
                                                "text": "Even last century scholars had begun to search for the cities in Asia Minor whose names have become so familiar to the Christian world through the Acts of the Apostles and the Epistles of St. Paul. Where were the places whose inhabitants received the famous Epistle to the Galatians?"
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "4dc64772-840c-3d72-9295-782ba5b99454",
                                        "entry_id": "2f1c175f-b608-30f7-9dea-53da9177f416",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "An extract from a New Testament epistle (sense 3.1) or book other than a gospel which is read during a church service, chiefly the Eucharist."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "d6274f23-21d2-40a1-a051-3bf522d15f92",
                                                "sense_id": "4dc64772-840c-3d72-9295-782ba5b99454",
                                                "text": "The Collectes ended, the prieſt, or he that is appointed, ſhall reade the Epiſtle, in a place aſſigned for the purpoſe, ſaying. The Epiſtle of ſainct Paule written in the [blank] Chapiter of [blank] to the."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "5888fa5d-2af1-442f-8756-e273504f6e9c",
                                                "sense_id": "4dc64772-840c-3d72-9295-782ba5b99454",
                                                "text": "EPISTLER, He vvho reads the Epiſtles in a Cathedral Church, &c."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            },
                            {
                                "target_word": "epistle",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "3f0214c3-df63-3583-b0e1-31e34f2a874b",
                                        "entry_id": "67ab9388-78a2-3f53-847b-4b51340b9e27",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "To write (something) in, or in the form of, a letter."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "humorous",
                                            "literary",
                                            "transitive"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "e98d8d56-bf7c-41fd-959e-c9bb2d7ff709",
                                                "sense_id": "3f0214c3-df63-3583-b0e1-31e34f2a874b",
                                                "text": "His braynes, his time, all hys maintenance and exhibition upon it he hath conſumed, and never intermitted, till ſuch time as he beganne to epiſtle it againſt mee, ſince which I have kept him a work indifferently."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "a4fdf0e2-9b1a-3a68-8006-16398d536d7a",
                                        "entry_id": "67ab9388-78a2-3f53-847b-4b51340b9e27",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "To write a letter to (someone)."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "archaic",
                                            "humorous",
                                            "literary",
                                            "transitive"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": []
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "95b30e3d-02cc-310e-a061-eba29f437a11",
                                        "entry_id": "67ab9388-78a2-3f53-847b-4b51340b9e27",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "To write (something) as an introduction or preface to a literary work; also, to provide (a literary work) with an introduction or preface."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "rare",
                                            "transitive"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "d93a3788-f50f-4f83-ab1a-9ccd0cca21a0",
                                                "sense_id": "95b30e3d-02cc-310e-a061-eba29f437a11",
                                                "text": "And though antient Tragedy uſe no Prologue, yet uſing ſometimes, in caſe of ſelf defence, or explanation, that vvhich Martial calls an Epiſtle; in behalf of this Tragedy coming forth after the antient manner, much different from vvhat among us paſſes for beſt, thus much before-hand may be Epiſtl'd; that Chorus is here introduc'd after the Greek manner, not antient only but modern, and ſtill in uſe among the Italians."
                                            }
                                        ]
